1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,460
Before you can animate something,

2
00:00:01,460 --> 00:00:03,380
you have to say where it is.

3
00:00:03,380 --> 00:00:04,600
So, quick question:

4
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,180
where is this dot?

5
00:00:06,180 --> 00:00:08,200
You might say: a bit to the right,

6
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:09,420
a little below the middle.

7
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:11,500
That is how people describe positions.

8
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:14,520
But a computer needs numbers,
and numbers need a ruler.

9
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:17,840
Manim's ruler is a grid
laid over the frame.

10
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,140
The centre of the screen is the origin.

11
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:22,340
And this dot is three units to the right,

12
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:23,460
and one unit down.

13
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:25,817
Three, minus one.

14
00:00:25,817 --> 00:00:28,377
The frame is eight units tall.

15
00:00:28,377 --> 00:00:31,117
Its width depends on
the shape of the video.

16
00:00:31,117 --> 00:00:35,297
For ordinary widescreen it works out
to about fourteen point two units.

17
00:00:35,297 --> 00:00:38,017
So anything between minus
seven and seven horizontally,

18
00:00:38,017 --> 00:00:40,257
and minus four and four vertically,

19
00:00:40,257 --> 00:00:41,977
is on screen.

20
00:00:41,977 --> 00:00:44,557
You could type coordinates
as lists of numbers,

21
00:00:44,557 --> 00:00:46,997
but Manim gives you something friendlier:

22
00:00:46,997 --> 00:00:48,317
direction constants.

23
00:00:48,317 --> 00:00:50,197
UP is one unit up.

24
00:00:50,197 --> 00:00:52,037
RIGHT is one unit right.

25
00:00:52,037 --> 00:00:56,037
And because they are vectors, arrows
with a length and a direction,

26
00:00:56,037 --> 00:00:58,357
you can do arithmetic with them.

27
00:00:58,357 --> 00:01:01,177
RIGHT times three is three units right.

28
00:01:01,177 --> 00:01:03,117
Add DOWN, and you arrive at three,

29
00:01:03,117 --> 00:01:08,637
minus one. Read it out loud and it sounds
like directions to a friend's house:

30
00:01:08,637 --> 00:01:11,577
go right three, then down one.

31
00:01:11,577 --> 00:01:16,937
That is why Manim code so often reads
like a description rather than a

32
00:01:16,937 --> 00:01:18,537
calculation.

33
00:01:18,537 --> 00:01:20,357
There are diagonal shortcuts too.

34
00:01:20,357 --> 00:01:23,097
U R means up and to the right,

35
00:01:23,097 --> 00:01:26,657
D L means down and to the left.

36
00:01:26,657 --> 00:01:30,637
You may have noticed each point
actually has three numbers.

37
00:01:30,637 --> 00:01:34,717
The third is depth,
pointing out of the screen.

38
00:01:34,717 --> 00:01:37,637
In flat scenes it is always zero,

39
00:01:37,637 --> 00:01:39,997
so you can ignore it until episode eight,

40
00:01:39,997 --> 00:01:42,397
where we will give it a job.

41
00:01:42,397 --> 00:01:47,517
This code plots four points with
labels written in exactly this style.

42
00:01:47,517 --> 00:01:49,683
Try adding a fifth.

43
00:01:49,683 --> 00:01:52,003
Manim comes with a whole zoo of shapes.

44
00:01:52,003 --> 00:01:54,223
Circles, squares, rectangles, triangles,

45
00:01:54,223 --> 00:01:56,663
regular polygons with as
many sides as you like,

46
00:01:56,663 --> 00:01:58,143
stars, lines, arrows and dots.

47
00:01:58,143 --> 00:01:59,983
Every one of them has sensible defaults.

48
00:01:59,983 --> 00:02:01,503
A circle has radius one.

49
00:02:01,503 --> 00:02:03,423
A square has sides of length two.

50
00:02:03,423 --> 00:02:06,743
A dot is tiny. And all of them
start out centred at the origin.

51
00:02:06,743 --> 00:02:08,623
Arguments let you override any of that:

52
00:02:08,623 --> 00:02:11,383
Circle with radius two, a
square with side length three,

53
00:02:11,383 --> 00:02:13,383
a regular polygon with six sides.

54
00:02:13,383 --> 00:02:16,263
Under the hood, nearly every shape
here is the same kind of thing:

55
00:02:16,263 --> 00:02:17,363
an outline made of curves.

56
00:02:17,363 --> 00:02:19,703
Episode four opens that box.

57
00:02:19,703 --> 00:02:21,783
For now, let's dress them up.

58
00:02:21,783 --> 00:02:24,643
Every shape has two
separate layers of paint.

59
00:02:24,643 --> 00:02:26,343
The stroke is the outline:

60
00:02:26,343 --> 00:02:28,463
it has a colour and a width.

61
00:02:28,463 --> 00:02:30,303
The fill is the inside:

62
00:02:30,303 --> 00:02:32,343
it has a colour and an opacity,

63
00:02:32,343 --> 00:02:35,543
where zero is invisible and one is solid.

64
00:02:35,543 --> 00:02:40,503
By default, most shapes are
outlines with an invisible fill.

65
00:02:40,503 --> 00:02:45,563
That is why the square in episode one
was hollow until we gave it a fill

66
00:02:45,563 --> 00:02:47,123
opacity.

67
00:02:47,123 --> 00:02:49,343
Set fill paints the inside.

68
00:02:49,343 --> 00:02:51,623
Set stroke paints the outline.

69
00:02:51,623 --> 00:02:54,103
Set color paints both at once.

70
00:02:54,103 --> 00:02:56,823
You can use named colours
like BLUE and YELLOW,

71
00:02:56,823 --> 00:03:01,003
or any hex code, like the
orange in this example.

72
00:03:01,003 --> 00:03:03,043
And notice how these calls are chained,

73
00:03:03,043 --> 00:03:04,983
one after another:

74
00:03:04,983 --> 00:03:07,923
Square, dot set fill, dot set stroke.

75
00:03:07,923 --> 00:03:13,123
That works because each method changes
the shape and then hands back the very

76
00:03:13,123 --> 00:03:17,083
same shape, so the next
method can keep working on it.

77
00:03:17,083 --> 00:03:21,917
You will see this chaining
style everywhere in Manim code.

78
00:03:21,917 --> 00:03:25,097
Once you have a shape, you will spend
a lot of time putting it somewhere.

79
00:03:25,097 --> 00:03:27,617
Manim gives you a handful
of verbs for this,

80
00:03:27,617 --> 00:03:31,917
and choosing the right one makes
code much easier to change later.

81
00:03:31,917 --> 00:03:33,517
Shift moves by an offset:

82
00:03:33,517 --> 00:03:35,777
wherever you are, go three units right.

83
00:03:35,777 --> 00:03:37,757
Move to goes to an absolute point:

84
00:03:37,757 --> 00:03:40,257
no matter where you started, end up here.

85
00:03:40,257 --> 00:03:42,597
Next to is the one you will use most.

86
00:03:42,597 --> 00:03:44,537
It places one object beside another,

87
00:03:44,537 --> 00:03:47,877
in a direction, with a
small gap called the buffer.

88
00:03:47,877 --> 00:03:49,537
How does it know where "beside" is?

89
00:03:49,537 --> 00:03:52,877
Every mobject has an
invisible bounding box,

90
00:03:52,877 --> 00:03:54,877
the smallest rectangle that contains it,

91
00:03:54,877 --> 00:03:57,317
and next to lines up those boxes.

92
00:03:57,317 --> 00:04:01,297
That is why labels stay neatly beside
things no matter how big they are.

93
00:04:01,297 --> 00:04:04,457
To edge and to corner push
an object against the frame,

94
00:04:04,457 --> 00:04:05,937
leaving a small margin.

95
00:04:05,937 --> 00:04:10,277
And align to lines up one edge of an
object with the same edge of another.

96
00:04:10,277 --> 00:04:12,957
You will notice something
new in these lines:

97
00:04:12,957 --> 00:04:16,717
dot animate. Putting animate in front
of a method turns that change into an

98
00:04:16,717 --> 00:04:18,617
animation, so instead of jumping,

99
00:04:18,617 --> 00:04:22,697
the dot glides. It is so useful it
gets a big part of next episode.

100
00:04:22,697 --> 00:04:23,857
A good rule of thumb:

101
00:04:23,857 --> 00:04:26,717
position things relative to
each other whenever you can.

102
00:04:26,717 --> 00:04:28,317
If you place a label with next to,

103
00:04:28,317 --> 00:04:29,737
and later move the shape,

104
00:04:29,737 --> 00:04:33,617
the label's relationship to it
is still written in your code.

105
00:04:33,617 --> 00:04:36,697
Moving is only one kind of change.

106
00:04:36,697 --> 00:04:38,877
Scale makes an object bigger or smaller,

107
00:04:38,877 --> 00:04:40,777
and rotate turns it.

108
00:04:40,777 --> 00:04:44,117
Both happen around the object's
centre unless you say otherwise,

109
00:04:44,117 --> 00:04:46,737
and you can pin a different
spot with about point,

110
00:04:46,737 --> 00:04:48,397
or with about edge,

111
00:04:48,397 --> 00:04:52,737
which keeps one side or corner fixed
while the rest grows away from it.

112
00:04:52,737 --> 00:04:54,997
Angles in Manim are measured in radians,

113
00:04:54,997 --> 00:04:59,557
so a full turn is two pi, and pi
over four is forty five degrees.

114
00:04:59,557 --> 00:05:01,357
If you'd rather think in degrees,

115
00:05:01,357 --> 00:05:03,017
multiply by the constant DEGREES:

116
00:05:03,017 --> 00:05:06,177
forty five times DEGREES
means exactly what it says.

117
00:05:06,177 --> 00:05:08,697
You can also ask an object
where its parts are.

118
00:05:08,697 --> 00:05:10,137
Get center returns its middle.

119
00:05:10,137 --> 00:05:11,317
Get top, get bottom,

120
00:05:11,317 --> 00:05:14,597
get left and get right return the
middle of each edge of its bounding box,

121
00:05:14,597 --> 00:05:16,537
and get corner returns a corner.

122
00:05:16,537 --> 00:05:18,697
And width and height
tell you how big it is.

123
00:05:18,697 --> 00:05:20,297
These answers are ordinary points,

124
00:05:20,297 --> 00:05:21,457
so you can hand them to move to,

125
00:05:21,457 --> 00:05:24,037
or to a Line, or use them in arithmetic.

126
00:05:24,037 --> 00:05:27,017
In fact, next to is built
from exactly these pieces:

127
00:05:27,017 --> 00:05:29,317
a point on one bounding box, a direction,

128
00:05:29,317 --> 00:05:31,067
and a buffer.

129
00:05:31,067 --> 00:05:34,187
Often you want to treat
several objects as one.

130
00:05:34,187 --> 00:05:36,147
That is what a V group is for.

131
00:05:36,147 --> 00:05:38,847
Put mobjects in a V
group, and you can move,

132
00:05:38,847 --> 00:05:42,767
scale, rotate, colour and
animate them all together.

133
00:05:42,767 --> 00:05:44,727
Groups can also organise their members.

134
00:05:44,727 --> 00:05:46,227
Arrange lines them up in a row,

135
00:05:46,227 --> 00:05:48,427
or a column, with even gaps.

136
00:05:48,427 --> 00:05:51,567
Arrange in grid lays them
out in rows and columns.

137
00:05:51,567 --> 00:05:55,227
In this example, twelve dots are
created with a single line of Python,

138
00:05:55,227 --> 00:05:56,607
a list comprehension,

139
00:05:56,607 --> 00:06:00,167
each with a colour a little further
along the way from blue to yellow.

140
00:06:00,167 --> 00:06:02,827
They all start at the origin,
stacked on top of each other,

141
00:06:02,827 --> 00:06:04,327
so at first you see only one.

142
00:06:04,327 --> 00:06:06,247
Then a single arrange spreads them out,

143
00:06:06,247 --> 00:06:08,487
and a single arrange in grid reflows them.

144
00:06:08,487 --> 00:06:10,287
And when we scale and rotate the group,

145
00:06:10,287 --> 00:06:13,867
every dot moves together, as if
they were glued to a sheet of glass.

146
00:06:13,867 --> 00:06:15,447
The group is an object in its own right,

147
00:06:15,447 --> 00:06:18,533
with its own centre and
its own bounding box.

148
00:06:18,533 --> 00:06:20,553
Now, the trap I promised.

149
00:06:20,553 --> 00:06:22,413
Here is a square called a.

150
00:06:22,413 --> 00:06:24,733
Suppose we write b equals a.

151
00:06:24,733 --> 00:06:26,653
How many squares are there?

152
00:06:26,653 --> 00:06:32,273
Just one. In Python, a name is like
a sticky note attached to an object.

153
00:06:32,273 --> 00:06:35,513
Writing b equals a does
not build a second square.

154
00:06:35,513 --> 00:06:37,673
It sticks a second note on the same one.

155
00:06:37,673 --> 00:06:40,193
So when we turn b red, a turns red too,

156
00:06:40,193 --> 00:06:42,953
because a and b are two
labels for one square.

157
00:06:42,953 --> 00:06:45,133
This surprises almost
everyone the first time,

158
00:06:45,133 --> 00:06:48,373
and it can cause some
very confusing animations.

159
00:06:48,373 --> 00:06:49,933
When you really want a second,

160
00:06:49,933 --> 00:06:52,233
independent object, ask for one:

161
00:06:52,233 --> 00:06:56,333
a dot copy. Copy builds a brand
new square with the same shape,

162
00:06:56,333 --> 00:07:02,000
position and style, and from then on the
two are free to go their separate ways.

163
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:03,340
One last property.

164
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:05,960
When shapes overlap,
which one is in front?

165
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:08,720
By default, the answer is
the order they were added:

166
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:11,300
later objects are painted
on top of earlier ones,

167
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:12,660
like layers of paper.

168
00:07:12,660 --> 00:07:14,160
When that is not what you want,

169
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:18,280
set z index. Objects with a
higher z index are drawn later,

170
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:21,660
so they appear in front,
regardless of when they were added.

171
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:26,750
It can even be animated, so a shape can
rise above its neighbours mid-scene.

172
00:07:26,750 --> 00:07:29,110
So here are the nouns of Manim.

173
00:07:29,110 --> 00:07:33,150
Positions are vectors, built from
directions like UP and RIGHT.

174
00:07:33,150 --> 00:07:35,070
Shapes have a stroke and a fill.

175
00:07:35,070 --> 00:07:37,510
You place them by offset,
by absolute point,

176
00:07:37,510 --> 00:07:39,430
or relative to each other.

177
00:07:39,430 --> 00:07:41,910
Groups let many objects act as one.

178
00:07:41,910 --> 00:07:44,590
Names are sticky notes, so
copy when you mean copy.

179
00:07:44,590 --> 00:07:47,770
And z index decides who is on top.

180
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:49,330
Next time: the verbs.

181
00:07:49,330 --> 00:07:51,890
How Manim moves from
one moment to the next,

182
00:07:51,890 --> 00:07:54,790
why some motions feel natural
and others feel robotic,

183
00:07:54,790 --> 00:07:58,450
and a strange thing that happens when
you rotate a square the wrong way.
